LOGBOOK.md:60:- GATES: Every M0 viability row maps evidence for both candidates. libssh2 has no waived red row; ReluxNIOSSH's missing neutral TCP ownership, opaque signer, bounded intake, algorithm reporting, keepalive, and unbuilt adapter rows remain explicit. The four deferred semantics remain unsupported/notReported under `TASK-260728-3cveay`; physical scale, extension memory, soak, and DNS footprint remain M3 gates.
LOGBOOK.md:63:- CONFIG GAP: Independent review found that positive-only rekey values did not satisfy the required range contract. The ADR now limits M0 composition to 4 KiB–5 GiB protected bytes, 100 ms–1 hour elapsed time, and the executed 10 s completion timeout. These are an admissible candidate envelope, not production defaults; `TASK-260715-1pn983` plus the M3 tuning/final matrix own final winners and any wider timeout range.
LOGBOOK.md:70:- STREAK: An initial output-suppressed aggregate streak reset at run 8 with exit 1 but retained no failing assertion; the immediate unsuppressed rerun passed. A fresh reset-on-any-failure streak with failure-line capture then passed 20/20 unfiltered `swift test --skip-build` runs, including historical keepalive run 13 and pressure run 17; every run passed 442 tests in 37 suites with only the 25 expected candidate-unavailable rows.
LOGBOOK.md:77:- VERIFICATION: The candidate-neutral suite and all 441 Swift tests pass; 25 known issues are explicit red ReluxNIOSSH rows because no production adapter target exists. Affected adapter coverage is 83.08% regions, 94.61% functions, and 92.11% lines. Format lint, core boundaries, and diff checks exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:84:### 2132 — Common SSH conformance runs the production adapter and preserves the absent candidate as red (TASK-260715-2d3g5e)
LOGBOOK.md:85:- TESTS: The candidate-neutral Swift Testing suite dispatches the same M0, cancellation-scope, and M3-deferred inventory from candidate data. The libssh2 row invokes `LibSSH2TransportFactory` through real OpenSSH/loopback adapter tests; ReluxNIOSSH has no `SSHTransport` adapter target and is represented by explicit known-issue rows rather than a green emulated fixture.
LOGBOOK.md:109:- ORCHESTRATION REWORK: Review found that the first helper only validated declarations. The checked-in provider now provisions task-owned current/fallback macOS sshd profiles, creates the no-mount/no-container Ubuntu Lima row, resolves the approved real-host alias, starts numeric-loopback destinations on each SSH server, enforces task-owned rotation versus real-host owner management, and tears everything down. Every non-success row requires a finite provider control before either candidate driver runs; free-form observation text is rejected. External provider commands remain optional overrides, while the two real candidate drivers stay external because their matrix tasks own candidate verdicts.
LOGBOOK.md:110:- VERIFICATION: The complete 26-test real-sshd libssh2 integration suite and full 428-test Swift suite pass, including server-side unapproved-key rejection, primary/fallback algorithms, host-policy ordering, channel rejection, M0 flows, and rekey-safe traffic. Thirty-three Python tests pass; live four-server lifecycle plus tests produce 83.7% line coverage for the fixture orchestrator and 88.3% for the provider. Integration tests prove real first-use/change/auth/channel failures, exact task-owned rotation, 128 KiB byte-exact transfer across a 32 KiB server rekey limit, endpoint behaviors, real latency/loss proxies, finite privacy-safe reports, and PID/state teardown. The full streamed 5 GiB source/sink reports exactly 5,368,709,120 bytes and `SHA256:fc01cfd7aebf90ff9491f8556131b6ef575c3e1fa33a0277ba28920bbaee7f54` without a payload file. Manifest/preflight validation, Python compilation/tab checks, strict Swift format lint, JSON parsing, and `git diff --check` exit 0. The live lifecycle reached Ubuntu 24.04/OpenSSH 9.6p1, macOS/OpenSSH 10.2p1 profiles, and the non-root real-host Darwin/OpenSSH 9.9p2 identity with public categorical output only.
LOGBOOK.md:169:- CONTRACT: The independently reviewed `TASK-260715-29ws8l` contract remains authoritative. Snapshot loading and production policy construction enforce at most one active approved identity; deterministic rotation publishes a complete newer snapshot with the former identity retained as an ordered revoked tombstone and one current active approval.
LOGBOOK.md:170:- VERIFICATION: Focused Swift Testing covers first use, same/different key, invalid label with identical bytes, malformed key, sequential rotation generations, one-active-approval validation, revocation, canonical-host/port mismatch, unsupported precedence, production composition, and redaction. A loopback libssh2 test proves all untrusted/rejected outcomes keep credential-provider calls, authentication attempts, and destination channels at zero.
LOGBOOK.md:175:- FORMAT AND PRIVACY: The strict `CredentialRecordV1` decoder enforces magic/version, exact reference and generation, bounds, trailing-byte rejection, optional-passphrase state, and constant-time digest comparison before an injected format registry constructs the external signer. Stable redacted errors distinguish not-provisioned, access-denied, wrong-class, generation, malformed, passphrase, unsupported-key, and cancellation outcomes. The accepted downstream key-format owner has not assigned production format IDs, so the default registry deliberately fails closed while imported/generated fixture registries exercise the candidate-neutral libssh2 seam.
LOGBOOK.md:238:- PRIVACY: Targeted post-ceremony scans found zero secret/key-path/key-ID/issuer-ID/account-credential candidate files in the Git-scoped repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history, and zero key files in repository/board scope. History nevertheless contains one safe Sparkle generation command-name line and one safe Keychain-unlock command-name line, with no password flags; full history suppression therefore cannot be claimed.
LOGBOOK.md:327:- EVIDENCE: The revised contract preserves and cites all three libssh2 stop-line packets, the ReluxNIOSSH adapter/window packet, the candidate audit, exact source locations, and accepted header/patch SHA-256 values. The original contract outcome and the preconditions for `TASK-260715-1ozsb6`, `TASK-260715-2d3g5e`, `TASK-260715-1u2vpc`, and `TASK-260728-3cveay` now carry the same revision.
LOGBOOK.md:455:- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
LOGBOOK.md:466:- FINDING: The reverse dependency stays forbidden and is what still kills the memory-only candidate B. Apple, same page: *"you should not rely on this communication channel with the containing app for basic Tunnel Provider operation (start, stop, etc.)."* App→provider may launch; provider→app may never be assumed. The transport selection (candidate D) therefore did not change — only the lifecycle around it.
LOGBOOK.md:543:- FINDING: Autonomy counts must be derived from each task's own text, not from a hand-kept hold list. Reading every reachable element's description/scope/AC surfaced six human nodes the previous two rounds missed: `2ayxqn` AC5 (owner acknowledges the P0 verdict), the three self-described MANUAL ratification checkpoints `1dsqnj`/`l639qp`/`2d308k`, and the release-candidate approvals `1r48pc`/`2aessv` plus the `yynqbr` freeze approval. Honest total: **226 autonomous agent tasks, 17 human-input nodes in 9 batches** — down from the 245/8 claimed in round 2.
